<problem>
<!-- <title>Integral of polynomial and exponential</title> -->

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="a" from="-10" to="10" exclude="0" numToSelect="5" withReplacement="true" />

<selectFromSequence name="aa" from="4" to="5" />
<selectFromSequence name="bb" from="2" to="3" />
<select name="cc">
  <option><number>0</number><math>1</math></option>
  <option><number>1</number><math>t</math></option>
</select>

<selectFromSequence name="lb" from="-2" to="1" />
<selectFromSequence name="ub" from="$lb+1" to="2" />


<function name="f" variables="t" symbolic="true">
  <math simplify="true">$a[1] *t^$aa +$a[2] *t^$bb +$a[3] *$cc[1][2]</math>
</function>
<function symbolic="true" simplify="numbers" name="indefinta" variables="t">
  <math simplify="numbers">$a[1] *t^($aa+1)/($aa+1)</math>
</function>
<function symbolic="true" simplify="numbers" name="indefintb" variables="t">
  <math simplify="numbers">$a[2] *t^($bb+1)/($bb+1)</math>
</function>
<function symbolic="true" simplify="numbers" name="indefintc" variables="t">
  <math simplify="numbers">$a[3] *t^($cc[1][1]+1)/($cc[1][1]+1)</math>
</function>

<function name="antideriv_f" variables="t" symbolic="true">
  <math simplify="numbers">$indefinta + $indefintb +$indefintc</math>
</function>


  
<math name="intfeval" simplify="numbers">$$antideriv_f($ub)-$$antideriv_f($lb)</math>

<math name="intaEval" simplify="numbers">$$indefinta($ub)-$$indefinta($lb)</math>
<math name="intbEval" simplify="numbers">$$indefintb($ub)-$$indefintb($lb)</math>
<math name="intcEval" simplify="numbers">$$indefintc($ub)-$$indefintc($lb)</math>
<math name="intaEvalSignError" simplify="numbers">$$indefinta($ub)+$$indefinta($lb)</math>
<math name="intbEvalSignError" simplify="numbers">$$indefintb($ub)+$$indefintb($lb)</math>
<math name="intcEvalSignError" simplify="numbers">$$indefintc($ub)+$$indefintc($lb)</math>

<math name="intf_signerror1a" simplify="numbers">
  $intaEvalSignError+$intbEval+$intcEval
</math>
<math name="intf_signerror1b" simplify="numbers">
  $intaEval+$intbEvalSignError+$intcEval
</math>
<math name="intf_signerror1c" simplify="numbers">
  $intaEval+$intbEval+$intcEvalSignError
</math>
<math name="intf_signerror2a" simplify="numbers">
  $intaEval+$intbEvalSignError+$intcEvalSignError
</math>
<math name="intf_signerror2b" simplify="numbers">
  $intaEvalSignError+$intbEval+$intcEvalSignError
</math>
<math name="intf_signerror2c" simplify="numbers">
  $intaEvalSignError+$intbEvalSignError+$intcEval
</math>
<math name="intf_signerror3" simplify="numbers">
  $intaEvalSignError+$intbEvalSignError+$intcEvalSignError
</math>

  

<!-- <math name='intseval' simplify='numbersPreserveOrder' > $a *($indefinta_ub &#x2D;$indefinta_lb )+$b *($indefintb_ub &#x2D;$indefintb_lb )+$c1 *($indefintc_ub &#x2D;$indefintc_lb ) </math> -->



  
<function name="g" variables="t" symbolic="true">
  <math simplify="numbers">$a[4] *e^($a[5] *t)</math>
</function>
<math simplify="full" name="intgeval">$a[4] *e^($a[5] *t)/$a[5]</math>

<math name="intgeval_C">$intgeval +C</math>
  
</setup>


<p>Evaluate the following integrals.  For full credit, do not round your answer to a decimal.  (Maximum of 90% credit will be awarded for rounded answers that include at least 5 correct significant digits.)</p>



<ol>

<li>
<m>\displaystyle \int_{$lb}^{$ub} ($f) dt =</m> 
<answer forceFullCheckworkButton="true">
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">$intfeval </award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.9" name="award6">$intfeval </award>
<award credit="0.9" name="award7">$intf_signerror1a </award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.81" name="award8">$intf_signerror1a </award>
<award credit="0.9" name="award9">$intf_signerror1b </award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.81" name="award10">$intf_signerror1b </award>
<award credit="0.9" name="award11">$intf_signerror1c </award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.81" name="award12">$intf_signerror1c </award>
<award credit="0.81" name="award13">$intf_signerror2a </award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.73" name="award14">$intf_signerror2a </award>
<award credit="0.81" name="award15">$intf_signerror2b </award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.73" name="award16">$intf_signerror2b </award>
<award credit="0.81" name="award17">$intf_signerror2c </award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.73" name="award18">$intf_signerror2c </award>
<award credit="0.73" name="award19">$intf_signerror3 </award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.66" name="award20">$intf_signerror3 </award>
</answer>
<feedback condition="$award6">Enter your answer as a fraction.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award7">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted one term of the integrand at the bounds.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award8">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted one term of the integrand at the bounds.  Enter your answer as a fraction.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award9">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted one term of the integrand at the bounds.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award10">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted one term of the integrand at the bounds.  Enter your answer as a fraction.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award11">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted one term of the integrand at the bounds.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award12">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted one term of the integrand at the bounds.  Enter your answer as a fraction.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award13">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted two terms of the integrand at the bounds.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award14">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted two terms of the integrand at the bounds.  Enter your answer as a fraction.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award15">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted two terms of the integrand at the bounds.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award16">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted two terms of the integrand at the bounds.  Enter your answer as a fraction.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award17">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted two terms of the integrand at the bounds.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award18">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted two terms of the integrand at the bounds.  Enter your answer as a fraction.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award19">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted three terms of the integrand at the bounds.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award20">It seems you may have added rather than subtracted three terms of the integrand at the bounds.  Enter your answer as a fraction.</feedback>


</li>

<li>
<m>\displaystyle \int $g \, dt =</m> 
<answer symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true" forceFullCheckworkButton="true">
  <award>$intgeval_C </award>
  <award credit="0.8" name="award0">$intgeval </award>
  <award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.9" name="award1">$intgeval_C </award>
  <award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.72" name="award2">$intgeval </award>
</answer>
<feedback condition="$award0">Don't forget the constant.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award1">Enter your answer as a fraction.</feedback>
<feedback condition="$award2">Enter your answer as a fraction, and don't forget the constant.</feedback>



</li>


</ol>


<solution>


<ol>

<li>
<p>We first use the sum and constant product rules for integration: 
</p>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \int_{$lb}^{$ub} ($f) dt  =  
  $a[1] \int_{$lb}^{$ub} t^$aa \, dt
  +$a[2] \int_{$lb}^{$ub} t^$bb \, dt 
  +$a[3] \int_{$lb}^{$ub} $cc[1][2] \, dt</m>
</p>
<p>
We then evaluate each of these integrals using the power rule for integration:
</p>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle $indefinta \Big|_{ $lb  }^{ $ub  }+$indefintb \Big|_{ $lb  }^{ $ub  }+$indefintc \Big|_{ $lb  }^{ $ub  }</m>
</p>
<p>
Finally, we substitute in the bounds and simplify:
</p>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle $intaEval+$intbEval+$intcEval  = $intfeval </m>
</p>

</li>

<li>
<p>
We first use the constant product rule for integration: 
</p>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \int $g \, dt  = $a[4] \int <math>e^($a[5] *t)</math> \, dt</m>
</p>
<p>
We then find the antiderivative: 
</p>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle $a[4] \left(<math>e^($a[5] *t)/$a[5]</math> \right)+C=$intgeval +C</m>
</p>
</li>


</ol>

</solution>


</problem>